cartridge "CHOPPER 1/M.SEDORE/1986"

' CHOPPER 1
' A ColecoVision game based on "Chopper 1" by Mike Sedore, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, August 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2076). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/chopper1.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a city at night with lit windows under a glowing
' force dome, a moon, a red helicopter with a turning rotor as a two-colour
' sprite, a grey saucer with chasing lights, plasma bombs, blasts, sounds.
'
' The game: an alien saucer patrols over the city and lets bombs go; it
' carries 100 a wave and can't be harmed. Fly the chopper and shoot the
' bombs with its pulse laser (it fires the way the chopper faces). A bomb
' that reaches the dome burns a hole in it; one that falls through a hole
' hits the city, which sinks a storey. Six hits destroy the city: no
' bonus, and the wave starts again. Stop the wave and the city is saved:
' 100 points for every storey left, and the next wave is faster. A bomb or
' the saucer itself wrecks the chopper; the pilot bails out, and while
' parachutes are left a new chopper comes. The pilot after the third
' parachute has none: GAME OVER.
'
' From the original (orig/): five bombs, each parked in the saucer until a
' random draw of 1 in 64 per bomb step lets it fall (while the wave has
' bombs left); a bomb shot, burnt out or landed waits 13 bomb steps before
' it is back in the saucer; the dome is one row of blocks, each stopping
' one bomb; a bomb that reaches the street sinks the whole city a row
' (6 rows, then CITY DESTROYED - NO BONUS, same wave again); 100 bombs a
' wave, the wave ends CITY SAVED! BONUS: storeys x 100; 10 points a bomb;
' 3 parachutes; a wrecked chopper hangs for 80 chopper steps, then the
' pilot drops off the screen and a new chopper starts in the middle;
' waves 1-9 chosen on the title (the bomb step slows by 1/17 of the wave-1
' delay per wave: delay 18 - wave); a shot hits a bomb within about 5
' pixels of its height and not still at the saucer. Speeds measured in
' jsc64 against the C64 clock and worked out per frame: bombs 0.58 C64
' pixels a frame at wave 1 (x 17 / (18 - wave)), the chopper 1.23, the
' saucer 0.47, the laser about 7.
'
' Liberties: the field is the ColecoVision's 208 x 152 instead of the C64's
' 232 x 190; bombs and the saucer move in screen pixels at the C64's
' speeds scaled to it (bombs 116/256 a frame at wave 1), the chopper and
' the laser keep their C64 speeds (1.25 and 6 pixels a frame); collisions
' are box tests (the C64 used its sprite collision registers); the wave
' ends when the saucer's last bomb has gone AND the sky is clear (the C64
' ended it the moment the 100th bomb fell, wiping the bombs in flight);
' the counted bonus and the jingles are new; the wave and START are chosen
' with the stick on the title (the C64: up/down and the button); no pause
' (the C64 paused on SHIFT); GAME OVER, then the title (the C64 waited for
' the button and started again); six-digit scores.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the chopper (it turns to
' face left or right), either button fires. On the title, up and down
' choose a line, left and right choose the wave, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

